reading: paired transfers conserve by construction, so this
must sit at the FP floor for EVERY lookahead. Anything else
means the transfer structure is wrong, not the scheduler.

== R2. CONVERGENCE — is it the SAME physics? ==
Comparing async(dt) to sync(dt) conflates two independent O(dt)
errors and cannot answer this. Both are measured instead against
a common fine-step reference: sync at dt0/64.
     dt    sync err     async err   sync ratio  async ratio
 0.02000   7.945e-06   1.200e-04       —            —
 0.01000   3.910e-06   5.867e-05       2.03x        2.05x
 0.00500   1.892e-06   3.024e-05       2.07x        1.94x
 0.00250   8.830e-07   1.488e-05       2.14x        2.03x
 0.00125   3.784e-07   7.204e-06       2.33x        2.07x

reading: BOTH columns must fall, at comparable order. That is
what 'same physics, different integrator' looks like. If the
async column plateaus while sync falls, the local scheme has a
different fixed point and is not adoptable.

== R3. DETERMINISM — bit-identity under reordering ==
  variant                        max|dtheta|   verdict
  canonical, reversed scan         0.000e+00   EXACT
  canonical, rotated origin 37     0.000e+00   EXACT
  canonical, rotated + reversed    0.000e+00   EXACT
  ARRIVAL order, reversed scan     1.214e-01   differs

reading: every canonical row must read EXACT — that property is
what lets the battery compare byte-identical reruns.

